\f4\fs26 \'93All that happened in a couple of days?\'94 Hayley had heard some stories in her time and had stumbled across some pretty bizarre circumstances, but she did not know what to think. \'93So you are not the terrorists that placed the bomb, but you are the idiots that redirected the water.\'94\
\'93Yes, but we fixed it and handed Shakti a suspect.\'94 Cracker decided to do all the confessing.\
\'93And you, Mr Hardy, are single-handedly responsible for the sudden change in the ethos of the entire Elvi nation? You turned them all from bunch of harmless musicians into rabid science freaks.\'94 The senior detective sank deeper into disbelief.\
\'93I guess. I was drunk and I told them a few things I had heard and they ran with it. Man, did they run with it.\'94 Cracker didn\'92t know what the big deal was. The songs they had written since he last spoke to them really kicked ass. Who doesn\'92t like songs about science and reason? Far better subject matter than cabbages and kings. \
\'93You have been helping Shakti run crime scenes, from a remote location. Anything else?\'94\
\'93Well, there\'92s the Discs and the death of Mr Lee, the disappearance of Emil Kalheri. The murder of the Director of Proactive Euthanasia seems awfully suspect, at least that\'92s what it seemed like from my vantage point.\'94\
\'93All of those matters will be re-investigated if I have any say. It seems obvious that Shakti was sent in by the Commissioner to balls up all three investigations, but to what end?\'94 Asked in the rhetoric, Hayley did not expect an answer and would ponder over the matter for the next couple of days.\
\'93What are you going to do with us?\'94 Sarah had heard confession was good for the soul, but had remained completely silent to this point, desperate to avoid disclosing the complete and awful truth. Unless it was completely unavoidable, she would not allow the truth of her viral infection to get out. The anguish and guilt she felt for exposing Cracker to the virus would, no doubt, increase exponentially should anyone else be infected. She was deeply moved Cracker was taking the heat from Hayley for her. He had clearly been interrogated a number of times before and seemed to know what areas to avoid, what matters to confess and when to make up some plausible alternate truth. \
\'93I should just drop you both in the shit and walk away, but I think that is exactly what the Commissioner wants. He sent Shakti after Mr Hardy clutching a brief with so many holes in it, he knew it could never proceed to conviction.\'94\
\'93That\'92s good isn\'92t it? I would have been acquitted anyway.\'94\
\'93I don\'92t think you get it. You were never going to be brought to trial. The Commissioner had you placed in custody in order to have you eliminated.\'94 Hayley announced as fact. \'93Once you are placed into processing, you will be completely out of my control. There is no way to protect you.\'94\
\'93If I am in danger, I suggest you get me the hell out of here.\'94 Cracker was frightened. He knew what cops were capable of and how they always weaseled their way out of trouble. They could kill him with virtually no questions asked.\
\'93I am trying to think of a safe-house that is actually safe, but nothing is coming to mind. We could put you in custody under an assumed name, perhaps.\'94\
\'93No thanks.\'94\
\'93I purchased an apartment in the management district a couple of months ago, the old apartment of Mr Lee actually, and I have not had the time to even inspect it. You can stay there, but you will have to be discrete, the neighbours will spot you as L.2 and file a report if you are not careful.\'94 Sarah had been waiting for an occupancy approval, but more recently she had found herself too preoccupied to follow it up. \
\'93Yes, I like it. The last place anyone will be looking for an L.2 is in the management district. We should leave as soon as possible.\'94 Hayley was now in close protection mode, a prisoner in her charge was in danger and it fell to her and Sarah to ensure his safety. No doubt the Commissioner has promised promotions for quietly hanging the Nightclub Vampire in his cell, knowing no-one would bother mourning the suicide of a killer obviously overwhelmed by guilt. \'93Getting out of the building may prove to be difficult. We should consider everyone we see as a threat. If we take him in the direction of the cell complex, we should not arouse suspicion until we break for the exit. You will have to wear cuffs, Cracker.\'94\
\'93Okay, but don\'92t lock them. I want to be able to defend myself.\'94 Cracker hated being handcuffed, even that time when Andrea was going through her kinky phase.\
As they stepped out of the interview suite, the chaos and noise of the main office instantaneously ceased. Every cop froze in place, hands resting on the gun handles, eyes slowly following them across the room and down towards the processing facility. A few followed, rubbing their chins, trying to work out how to shoot the three of them in the back and make it look like self-defence. Hayley, Sarah and Cracker upped the pace, took a left turn where they should have turned right and broke into a sprint. \
 \'93Looks like an open contract, Cracker.\'94 Hayley thought only a discrete few would have been in on the kill, but from the interest they had already received, the promotion opportunity was open to all and sundry. \'93We had better think of another plan, quickly.\'94\
The passageway was blocked by a giant metal security door, but it may as well have been made of paper, such was the speed at which Sarah defeated the electronics. The exit door lay ahead, but Hayley\'92s heart sank when she saw it required a access chip to go further.\
\'93We are trapped.\'94 Hayley could hear their pursuers, now a small mob, trundling down the corridor towards them. \'93They are getting close, sorry Cracker.\'94\
\'93Not so.\'94 Sarah put her hand in her jacket pocket and pulled out the company \'91all access chip\'92 she had been carrying since she first met Cracker and opened the door without fuss. \'93I knew this would come in handy eventually.\'94\
\'93Alright, something is finally going right for us.\'94 Cracker said with relief. The doors slid open, very wide and they found their escape was blocked by a line of 10 or so uniforms, pistols drawn, in firing squad configuration. A senior sergeant walked forward from the line. \'93This man is trying to escape, Superintendent, I suggest you and your girlfriend step back.\'94\

